# Trivy CVE exemptions — narrow, per-CVE, with written reason + upgrade plan.
#
# Every entry is a comment block immediately above a bare CVE/GHSA id, and it
# MUST carry all three of these fields, spelled exactly like this:
#
#   # CVE-YYYY-NNNNN — one-line description of the advisory
#   #   Affected path:  the path Trivy reports for the vulnerable package
#   #   Why exempt:     why this is safe in THIS image, today
#   #   Upgrade plan:   the concrete trigger that retires this entry
#   CVE-YYYY-NNNNN
#
# `Affected path` is not decoration: it is what lets a guard check the
# exemption still has a subject. An entry naming a path the Dockerfile
# deletes suppresses nothing, and is deleted rather than kept "just in case".
#
# Adding entries here is a structural escape hatch for the Trivy gate. Use
# sparingly: prefer fixing the dep over ignoring it.
#
# Enforced by `tests/guards/trivyignore-exemptions.test.ts`.

# ── No active exemptions ──
#
# All eight previous entries exempted CVEs whose affected path was
# `usr/local/lib/node_modules/npm/node_modules/...`. Dockerfile:163 now
# deletes that directory outright, so those packages are absent from the
# image Trivy scans and the exemptions matched nothing. The Dockerfile's
# own comment states the intent: "Deleting the CLI removes the finding at
# the source rather than suppressing it in .trivyignore."
#
# Their stated retirement trigger — "the next Node base-image bump that
# ships an npm CLI bundling ..." — had also become unreachable, since the
# npm CLI is no longer in the image at any version.
